WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.020
Here's a question that nobody in the world knows the answer to.

00:00:03.260 --> 00:00:05.468
Suppose you have some closed continuous curve,

00:00:05.468 --> 00:00:09.644
which essentially means some squiggle you could draw on paper without lifting the pen,

00:00:09.644 --> 00:00:10.940
that ends where it starts.

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:16.087
If you can find four points somewhere on this loop that make the vertices of a square,

00:00:16.087 --> 00:00:18.540
it's called an inscribed square of the loop.

00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:22.361
And the unsolved question is whether every possible closed

00:00:22.361 --> 00:00:26.120
continuous loop like this necessarily has an inscribed square.

00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:30.657
The question was originally posed by Otto Toeplitz in 1911,

00:00:30.657 --> 00:00:33.880
and it's commonly known as the inscribed square problem.

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:38.020
One of my all-time favorite pieces of math, easily in the top five,

00:00:38.020 --> 00:00:41.664
is a very beautiful proof for a simpler version of this question,

00:00:41.664 --> 00:00:45.640
asking instead whether you can necessarily find an inscribed rectangle.

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:49.933
The argument is originally due to Herbert Vaughan, and as a spoiler,

00:00:49.933 --> 00:00:53.944
our discussion here is going to lead you to think about this shape right here,

00:00:53.944 --> 00:00:58.260
what's known as a Klein bottle, but it won't come up as some curiosity or a trinket.

00:00:58.660 --> 00:01:01.260
It arises as a natural problem-solving tool.

00:01:02.120 --> 00:01:06.100
One of the earliest videos that I made for this channel was actually about this proof,

00:01:06.100 --> 00:01:09.200
and I'm making this video as a kind of second edition to that one.

00:01:09.500 --> 00:01:12.768
I realize second editions are much less of a thing for YouTube videos than

00:01:12.768 --> 00:01:15.948
they are for books, but part of my motivation here is that there's been

00:01:15.948 --> 00:01:19.260
more research since that original video that's very much worth discussing.

00:01:19.740 --> 00:01:23.768
Another motivation is that there's numerous caveats and interesting connections

00:01:23.768 --> 00:01:27.440
both beautiful and mind-broadening that are definitely worth including.

00:01:27.780 --> 00:01:30.618
Aside from that, given that it's one of my favorite pieces of math,

00:01:30.618 --> 00:01:34.346
I kind of just want to take a stab at animating it and motivating each one of the steps

00:01:34.346 --> 00:01:35.320
as best as I know how.

00:01:36.680 --> 00:01:39.948
Some of you might ask why on earth anyone would care about

00:01:39.948 --> 00:01:43.160
proving that any closed loop has an inscribed rectangle.

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:45.300
I personally don't know of any application.

00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:47.480
Frankly, I would be surprised if there was one.

00:01:47.780 --> 00:01:50.852
But I think many of you know that engaging with challenging puzzles,

00:01:50.852 --> 00:01:54.330
even when they're pure puzzles, has a way of sharpening your problem-solving

00:01:54.330 --> 00:01:58.080
instincts in a way that can be carried over to other practical applications later.

00:01:58.180 --> 00:02:02.200
But I can also give you a much more specific reason why I love this particular proof.

00:02:02.580 --> 00:02:06.254
It's something I first saw a long time ago, and I remember it being the

00:02:06.254 --> 00:02:09.979
first time that I felt a sense for what topology is actually all about.

00:02:10.440 --> 00:02:12.990
You see, in a lot of recreational math settings,

00:02:12.990 --> 00:02:17.080
topology is sometimes presented as something like a study of bizarre shapes.

00:02:17.080 --> 00:02:21.043
A common classroom activity might be to apply a half-twist to a thin piece

00:02:21.043 --> 00:02:25.060
of paper and glue the ends together, forming what's called a Möbius strip.

00:02:25.720 --> 00:02:28.760
You might be told this is what's called a non-orientable surface.

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:32.600
Loosely speaking, this means there's no notion of an inner side or an outer side.

00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:34.540
The whole thing just has one side.

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:38.570
Topology is also often presented as a sort of rubber sheet geometry,

00:02:38.570 --> 00:02:42.770
where shapes are considered the same if you can deform one into another without

00:02:42.770 --> 00:02:43.400
tearing it.

00:02:43.400 --> 00:02:47.280
Neither of these notions, in my opinion, really captures what it's actually about.

00:02:47.800 --> 00:02:49.719
And when I was growing up, I remember these kinds

00:02:49.719 --> 00:02:51.520
of examples left a frustrating open question.

00:02:52.060 --> 00:02:52.900
How is this math?

00:02:53.200 --> 00:02:55.240
How does any of this actually help you solve problems?

00:02:55.900 --> 00:03:00.083
If you stick with me to the end here, you'll see how these shapes and their bizarre

00:03:00.083 --> 00:03:04.166
properties are not just idle curiosities, but they're actual tools for logic and

00:03:04.166 --> 00:03:04.720
deduction.

00:03:07.260 --> 00:03:10.197
The first step in proving that any closed loop contains a

00:03:10.197 --> 00:03:13.700
rectangle is going to be to reframe the question just a little bit.

00:03:14.060 --> 00:03:18.102
Instead of thinking about four points that are the vertices of a rectangle,

00:03:18.102 --> 00:03:21.120
think about searching for two distinct pairs of points,

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:25.054
such that the lines connecting each pair have the same midpoint and they

00:03:25.054 --> 00:03:26.240
have the same length.

00:03:27.140 --> 00:03:29.429
Hopefully it's not too hard to convince yourself that this

00:03:29.429 --> 00:03:31.600
really is the same thing as searching for a rectangle.

00:03:32.200 --> 00:03:35.857
If I told you, hey, I found two line segments somewhere out in space,

00:03:35.857 --> 00:03:39.143
and I further specify that both of them have the same center,

00:03:39.143 --> 00:03:41.793
and also that both segments have the same length,

00:03:41.793 --> 00:03:45.980
then the four endpoints of those two different lines have to form a rectangle.

00:03:46.440 --> 00:03:49.220
If you want, you could try to pause and ponder to rigorously prove this.

00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:51.960
It's a relatively straightforward geometry exercise.

00:03:52.640 --> 00:03:56.746
Given some arbitrary closed loop, what you and I are going to do

00:03:56.746 --> 00:04:00.980
is somehow think about all possible pairs of points on that loop.

00:04:01.400 --> 00:04:03.820
For any one of these pairs, we care about two things.

00:04:04.040 --> 00:04:06.502
The first is where its midpoint sits, which you

00:04:06.502 --> 00:04:08.860
might think of as two numbers worth of data.

00:04:09.100 --> 00:04:12.480
The xy coordinates on the plane where the loop sits.

00:04:12.860 --> 00:04:15.694
The other thing we care about is the distance between those points,

00:04:15.694 --> 00:04:16.920
which is another data point.

00:04:17.440 --> 00:04:22.117
Now if you're a mathematician and you see three numbers worth of information like this,

00:04:22.117 --> 00:04:26.148
it is a very natural step to try packaging them together and think of that

00:04:26.148 --> 00:04:29.320
data as being a single point in a three-dimensional space.

00:04:29.860 --> 00:04:34.569
In our example, if you imagine the loop sitting on an xy plane inside that space,

00:04:34.569 --> 00:04:39.279
and its midpoint has some coordinates xy, then this 3d point that we care about,

00:04:39.279 --> 00:04:44.221
the one packaging x, y, and d, could be thought about as a point directly above that

00:04:44.221 --> 00:04:49.337
midpoint, such that the distance off the plane matches the distance between the pair of

00:04:49.337 --> 00:04:50.500
points on the loop.

00:04:51.040 --> 00:04:53.549
Some other pair of points on the loop would correspond

00:04:53.549 --> 00:04:55.780
to some other point in three-dimensional space.

00:04:56.340 --> 00:04:58.620
And in essence, what we have here is a mapping.

00:04:58.980 --> 00:05:02.620
A mapping from pairs of points on the loop to three-dimensional space.

00:05:03.040 --> 00:05:07.060
The important feature of this mapping that we're going to rely on is that it's continuous.

00:05:07.720 --> 00:05:11.060
And essentially what this means is if you just slightly wiggle the input,

00:05:11.060 --> 00:05:14.720
slightly nudging that pair of points, the output only slightly wiggles as well.

00:05:15.120 --> 00:05:16.440
There are never any sudden jumps.

00:05:16.900 --> 00:05:20.419
In our search for inscribed rectangles, with respect to this mapping,

00:05:20.419 --> 00:05:24.704
it amounts to searching for a kind of coincidence when two distinct pairs of points

00:05:24.704 --> 00:05:28.886
map to the same output, since by definition that would mean they have to have the

00:05:28.886 --> 00:05:32.253
same midpoint and be the same distance apart, which, like I said,

00:05:32.253 --> 00:05:34.140
means they have to form a rectangle.

00:05:34.820 --> 00:05:37.598
So if you imagine yourself as a mathematician mulling this over,

00:05:37.598 --> 00:05:40.940
you want to know how you can prove that this kind of collision would happen.

00:05:41.500 --> 00:05:45.316
Looking over all possible pairs of points on the loop and all their corresponding

00:05:45.316 --> 00:05:49.320
three-dimensional outputs, how could you know for sure that a collision must happen?

00:05:50.780 --> 00:05:54.843
You might complain that this all feels like stating the same question just in more

00:05:54.843 --> 00:05:58.856
complicated language, but where it really starts to feel qualitatively different

00:05:58.856 --> 00:06:02.770
is if I invite you to think about the set of all possible outputs of this map,

00:06:02.770 --> 00:06:07.180
every possible point in 3D space that corresponds to some pair of loop points like this.

00:06:07.780 --> 00:06:10.427
Taken together, they form this kind of wild surface,

00:06:10.427 --> 00:06:14.040
which looks like some incredibly beautiful but complicated Frank Gehry

00:06:14.040 --> 00:06:15.160
architectural design.

00:06:15.600 --> 00:06:18.148
Even when I render this out, personally I find it kind of

00:06:18.148 --> 00:06:20.920
hard to even wrap my mind around what exactly I'm looking at.

00:06:21.100 --> 00:06:23.320
What I found helpful was to look at cross-sections.

00:06:25.280 --> 00:06:28.919
Near the bottom, the cross-sections of this surface look approximately like

00:06:28.919 --> 00:06:32.800
the loop itself, which is a surprisingly important fact that we'll get back to.

00:06:33.200 --> 00:06:35.730
And then let me pull up those two pairs of points again and

00:06:35.730 --> 00:06:38.260
position them so that they sit on some rectangle vertices.

00:06:38.560 --> 00:06:42.926
Then if I bring up the cross-section to the height of that corresponding output,

00:06:42.926 --> 00:06:44.400
notice what it looks like.

00:06:45.960 --> 00:06:50.460
Around that point, it kind of feels like the surface is crossing through itself.

00:06:51.160 --> 00:06:55.790
And in fact, for the example I'm showing, there's not just one point of intersection,

00:06:55.790 --> 00:06:59.712
there's this continuous curve of self-intersection points corresponding

00:06:59.712 --> 00:07:02.980
to a continuous family of inscribed rectangles in the loop.

00:07:03.540 --> 00:07:05.960
This is a really cool way to think about inscribed rectangles.

00:07:06.420 --> 00:07:10.132
Every point you see in the surface that feels like self-intersection

00:07:10.132 --> 00:07:13.680
in this way corresponds to some inscribed rectangle in the loop.

00:07:16.320 --> 00:07:20.398
So far, all of this has just been illustrated with a single example loop,

00:07:20.398 --> 00:07:24.029
but keep in mind, every possible loop that you could draw on the

00:07:24.029 --> 00:07:27.660
plane has its own corresponding unique surface sitting above it.

00:07:28.180 --> 00:07:31.186
Proving that this kind of self-intersection has to happen no

00:07:31.186 --> 00:07:34.243
matter what loop you start with really comes down to getting

00:07:34.243 --> 00:07:37.600
to know this surface and who it really is and what it's all about.

00:07:38.560 --> 00:07:41.030
In the first edition of this lesson, many commenters asked,

00:07:41.030 --> 00:07:43.040
what would this surface look like for a circle?

00:07:43.780 --> 00:07:46.400
And in this case, the result is deceptively simple.

00:07:46.600 --> 00:07:47.720
It looks something like a dome.

00:07:48.080 --> 00:07:50.820
And at first, this might seem like it has no self-intersection.

00:07:51.480 --> 00:07:55.001
But the imperfections with how the surface is being rendered do give a little

00:07:55.001 --> 00:07:58.660
bit of a hint that something funny is happening near the very top of that dome.

00:07:59.220 --> 00:08:02.860
And if you think about it, a circle certainly has inscribed rectangles.

00:08:03.060 --> 00:08:04.960
In fact, it has infinitely many of them.

00:08:05.280 --> 00:08:08.320
And all of them have a midpoint at the center of that circle.

00:08:08.800 --> 00:08:11.224
And the length of the diagonal for those rectangles

00:08:11.224 --> 00:08:13.220
is always equal to the circle's diameter.

00:08:13.800 --> 00:08:17.329
So what this means is that the function we define from pairs of

00:08:17.329 --> 00:08:20.635
loop points into 3D space maps many many different points,

00:08:20.635 --> 00:08:24.220
infinitely many, onto this single point on the top of the dome.

00:08:24.780 --> 00:08:28.200
So we certainly have the collision that we're looking for, infinitely many in fact.

00:08:28.540 --> 00:08:30.976
But for this example, it doesn't have that same look of

00:08:30.976 --> 00:08:33.500
a ghostly sheet kind of passing through itself in space.

00:08:34.059 --> 00:08:37.694
If I squish the circle to become an ellipse, you can see how that single

00:08:37.694 --> 00:08:41.480
point of many intersections becomes a vertical line of self-intersections.

00:08:41.940 --> 00:08:45.360
And I'll invite you to pause and ponder to think about why that might be the case.

00:08:45.360 --> 00:08:48.958
The point is, just keep in mind when I use this phrase, self-intersection,

00:08:48.958 --> 00:08:53.188
what I'm really getting at is the idea of two different pairs of points mapping to the

00:08:53.188 --> 00:08:53.820
same output.

00:08:54.360 --> 00:08:57.590
In almost all cases, that looks like a surface passing through itself,

00:08:57.590 --> 00:08:59.620
but it's possible for it to look different.

00:09:00.360 --> 00:09:02.751
Another point that's maybe worth emphasizing is that

00:09:02.751 --> 00:09:05.280
the surface you're looking at is not a function graph.

00:09:05.800 --> 00:09:08.864
A function graph in three dimensions comes from functions that

00:09:08.864 --> 00:09:11.780
have two numbers as an input and one number as the output.

00:09:12.220 --> 00:09:14.260
Right here we have something notably more complicated.

00:09:14.260 --> 00:09:17.706
The inputs of the function are pairs of points on the loop,

00:09:17.706 --> 00:09:21.560
and the outputs are triplets of numbers, full points in 3D space.

00:09:21.920 --> 00:09:25.240
What you're looking at is the set of all outputs, it's not a graph.

00:09:26.020 --> 00:09:29.865
There's a famous quote from the author Anton Chekhov giving advice about writing,

00:09:29.865 --> 00:09:33.236
where he says, if in the first act you have hung a pistol on the wall,

00:09:33.236 --> 00:09:35.420
then in the following one it should be fired.

00:09:35.940 --> 00:09:39.803
His main point is about only including strictly necessary information,

00:09:39.803 --> 00:09:44.272
but another principle, implicit, is that the seeds for dramatic action should be

00:09:44.272 --> 00:09:45.100
planted early.

00:09:45.840 --> 00:09:49.199
In our story here about topology and proving inscribed rectangles,

00:09:49.199 --> 00:09:52.660
I want to draw your attention to a certain gun hanging on the wall.

00:09:53.140 --> 00:09:55.901
I mentioned how near the plane of the curve, the cross sections

00:09:55.901 --> 00:09:58.400
of the surface look approximately like the curve itself.

00:09:59.020 --> 00:09:59.760
Why is that?

00:10:00.480 --> 00:10:03.300
Well, think about a pair of points that are really close together.

00:10:03.660 --> 00:10:07.661
Because the distance between them is small, the output has a very small z-coordinate,

00:10:07.661 --> 00:10:10.580
and the midpoint is close to both of those points themselves.

00:10:10.580 --> 00:10:15.027
In the extreme, if you have a pair of points that's really just the same spot on the

00:10:15.027 --> 00:10:19.580
curve, listed twice, then the output of this mapping is that same point on the curve.

00:10:20.220 --> 00:10:20.780
Remember that.

00:10:21.180 --> 00:10:25.840
All pairs that look like x,x correspond to points on the curve itself.

00:10:27.140 --> 00:10:30.554
So we have this wild surface, we're trying to prove self-intersections,

00:10:30.554 --> 00:10:34.305
and the next step for how we're going to do this is to come up with a second,

00:10:34.305 --> 00:10:38.440
very natural way to associate pairs of points on a loop with a certain other surface.

00:10:38.440 --> 00:10:43.358
The final argument is then going to come down to understanding how this other mystery

00:10:43.358 --> 00:10:48.160
surface can or cannot be embedded into three dimensions without self-intersection.

00:10:49.600 --> 00:10:53.657
To set this up, it helps to give the loop a kind of internal coordinate system,

00:10:53.657 --> 00:10:57.560
let's say associating each point on the loop with a number between 0 and 1.

00:10:58.240 --> 00:11:01.940
Geometrically, an association like this is a little bit like snipping the loop at

00:11:01.940 --> 00:11:05.640
some point and then flattening it out onto the unit interval of the number line.

00:11:05.640 --> 00:11:11.222
Every point of the loop is associated with a unique number from 0 to 1, and vice versa,

00:11:11.222 --> 00:11:16.675
with the single exception of the fact that both 0 and 1 map to the same point of the

00:11:16.675 --> 00:11:17.060
loop.

00:11:17.500 --> 00:11:20.760
So for this to be a continuous association in both directions,

00:11:20.760 --> 00:11:25.073
something we're going to care a lot about, you might imagine wanting to glue that

00:11:25.073 --> 00:11:26.440
number 0 to the number 1.

00:11:26.740 --> 00:11:28.400
You'll see in a moment why this is important.

00:11:28.940 --> 00:11:31.835
Now of course we don't care about single points on the loop,

00:11:31.835 --> 00:11:34.681
our whole study here is about pairs of points on the loop,

00:11:34.681 --> 00:11:38.444
and if you have some second point, naturally you might think of a second unit

00:11:38.444 --> 00:11:39.940
interval maybe along a y-axis.

00:11:40.620 --> 00:11:43.590
So here the x-axis gives a coordinate for the first point,

00:11:43.590 --> 00:11:46.509
and the y-axis gives a coordinate for that second point,

00:11:46.509 --> 00:11:50.658
and the pair of points taken together would correspond to a single point in this

00:11:50.658 --> 00:11:54.959
unit square, where the two-dimensional coordinates of that point essentially encode

00:11:54.959 --> 00:11:57.520
which two points on the loop we're talking about.

00:11:57.980 --> 00:12:03.233
This association between individual points in the square and pairs of points on the loop

00:12:03.233 --> 00:12:08.306
is almost a continuous one-to-one map, but again there's some awkwardness around the

00:12:08.306 --> 00:12:13.380
edges based on the fact that the coordinates 0 and 1 really describe the same thing.

00:12:13.800 --> 00:12:18.118
For example, look at all of the points on the left here where the x-coordinate is 0,

00:12:18.118 --> 00:12:20.020
corresponding to this vertical line.

00:12:20.560 --> 00:12:24.867
All those points really represent the same loop pair as the ones on the right side,

00:12:24.867 --> 00:12:27.980
where the x-coordinate is 1, this other vertical line here.

00:12:28.300 --> 00:12:30.169
I'll color both of them blue and I'll put some

00:12:30.169 --> 00:12:32.200
arrows on there to help remember the orientation.

00:12:32.700 --> 00:12:37.059
Similarly, all of the points whose y-coordinate is 0, this bottom line here,

00:12:37.059 --> 00:12:41.820
represent the same loop pairs as the point whose y-coordinate is 1, up at the top.

00:12:42.760 --> 00:12:46.027
If you're thinking topologically, you don't just want to record this,

00:12:46.027 --> 00:12:48.300
you want to somehow represent it geometrically.

00:12:48.680 --> 00:12:52.260
And the way we'll do this is to glue both of those blue lines together,

00:12:52.260 --> 00:12:54.580
which you might think of as giving this tube.

00:12:54.900 --> 00:12:58.842
The green lines have now turned into these circles at the end of the tube,

00:12:58.842 --> 00:13:02.784
and to glue those together it requires curling the tube around on itself,

00:13:02.784 --> 00:13:06.780
giving us this surface of a donut shape, which is commonly called a torus.

00:13:07.740 --> 00:13:10.505
And I want to point out it's not like we landed here because

00:13:10.505 --> 00:13:13.040
of some random pondering about donuts and coffee cups.

00:13:13.500 --> 00:13:16.349
The surface seems to be a really natural representation

00:13:16.349 --> 00:13:18.680
of all possible pairs of points on the loop.

00:13:19.100 --> 00:13:22.180
And what I mean when I say natural here is essentially two things.

00:13:22.520 --> 00:13:24.120
First of all, the mapping goes both ways.

00:13:24.700 --> 00:13:28.115
Every point of the torus corresponds to a unique pair of points on the loop,

00:13:28.115 --> 00:13:31.440
and each pair of points on the loop gives us a unique point on the torus.

00:13:32.200 --> 00:13:36.539
Secondly, the association is continuous, meaning if I wiggle some point on the torus,

00:13:36.539 --> 00:13:40.827
it results in only a slight wiggle to the corresponding pair of points on the loop,

00:13:40.827 --> 00:13:43.380
and vice versa, there are never any sudden jumps.

00:13:43.960 --> 00:13:47.440
But actually, the torus is not quite the right surface for our purposes.

00:13:48.100 --> 00:13:49.540
Remember why we're doing any of this.

00:13:49.660 --> 00:13:52.630
We're looking for two distinct pairs of points on an arbitrary

00:13:52.630 --> 00:13:55.600
loop that have the same midpoint and the same distance apart.

00:13:56.080 --> 00:13:59.440
But it matters whether we care about the order of a pair of points.

00:13:59.440 --> 00:14:03.224
If you consider the pair a,b to be distinct from the pair b,a,

00:14:03.224 --> 00:14:07.191
then that would give you a trivial example of two distinct pairs

00:14:07.191 --> 00:14:11.280
of points that have the same midpoint and the same distance apart.

00:14:11.680 --> 00:14:14.205
But this is like saying that you can always find an inscribed

00:14:14.205 --> 00:14:17.020
rectangle as long as you're okay with an infinitely thin rectangle.

00:14:17.480 --> 00:14:21.313
Clearly what we want is to prove that there are non-trivial rectangles,

00:14:21.313 --> 00:14:25.255
and for that you need to think of the pair of points as being unordered,

00:14:25.255 --> 00:14:28.980
in the sense that a,b and b,a should really refer to the same thing.

00:14:29.440 --> 00:14:33.116
Looking back at our unit square, you now want to somehow record

00:14:33.116 --> 00:14:36.676
the fact that every point with coordinates x,y should really

00:14:36.676 --> 00:14:40.060
be considered the same as the point with coordinates y,x.

00:14:40.940 --> 00:14:44.867
If you take a moment to think about it, what you essentially want here is that

00:14:44.867 --> 00:14:48.694
all of the points on the square that are reflected along this diagonal line

00:14:48.694 --> 00:14:52.420
should be considered as representing the same pair of points on the loop.

00:14:52.900 --> 00:14:55.378
And again, you can think of this with a kind of gluing,

00:14:55.378 --> 00:14:58.217
where we're going to fold the square along that diagonal line,

00:14:58.217 --> 00:15:00.020
kind of like a grilled cheese sandwich.

00:15:00.660 --> 00:15:04.200
And when you do this, I want to draw your attention again to that gun hanging on the wall.

00:15:04.880 --> 00:15:09.526
Every pair of points that looks like x,x corresponds to a point somewhere on the crease

00:15:09.526 --> 00:15:14.173
of this fold, this diagonal line, which from this point on I'm going to be coloring in

00:15:14.173 --> 00:15:14.440
red.

00:15:15.240 --> 00:15:18.273
You still need to glue the edges together, as a way to remember

00:15:18.273 --> 00:15:20.920
that 0 and 1 really map to the same point on the loop.

00:15:21.320 --> 00:15:24.560
But after our diagonal fold, we're faced with a real puzzle here.

00:15:24.880 --> 00:15:25.900
Notice the orientation.

00:15:26.460 --> 00:15:30.320
How exactly to do this is puzzling enough to almost feel contradictory.

00:15:31.040 --> 00:15:35.160
But sometimes with topology, you have to take a step back in order to take a step forward.

00:15:35.640 --> 00:15:38.613
The trick here is to cut this piece along another diagonal,

00:15:38.613 --> 00:15:42.291
and we're going to add some new arrows to remember to glue that cut back

00:15:42.291 --> 00:15:43.400
together in a moment.

00:15:43.840 --> 00:15:46.888
Doing this lets you glue together the original two arrows,

00:15:46.888 --> 00:15:50.620
which gives us this square with two edges that still need to be glued.

00:15:50.960 --> 00:15:54.280
But notice that the orientation of the remaining arrows are reversed.

00:15:54.280 --> 00:15:58.621
So to glue those back together, what you need to do is somehow introduce a

00:15:58.621 --> 00:16:03.080
single half twist, and this is exactly the construction for a Möbius strip.

00:16:03.720 --> 00:16:05.140
That is really cool to me.

00:16:05.520 --> 00:16:07.982
And again, I want to emphasize this is not some

00:16:07.982 --> 00:16:10.340
arbitrary play task with construction paper.

00:16:10.620 --> 00:16:12.720
This shape arose for us very naturally.

00:16:13.120 --> 00:16:18.040
It's a way to geometrically represent all possible unordered pairs of points on a loop.

00:16:18.500 --> 00:16:22.610
And again, what I mean by natural here is that each point of the strip corresponds

00:16:22.610 --> 00:16:26.720
to a unique pair on the loop, and vice versa, and the relationship is continuous.

00:16:27.220 --> 00:16:30.120
Small nudges on one side correspond to small nudges on the other.

00:16:30.620 --> 00:16:34.580
The other thing I want to draw your attention to is the red edge of that Möbius strip.

00:16:35.160 --> 00:16:38.680
Originally, this is what came from the diagonal in that unit square.

00:16:39.040 --> 00:16:42.540
It represents all pairs of points that look like x comma x.

00:16:42.920 --> 00:16:45.920
That is, pairs that are really the same point but just listed twice.

00:16:47.400 --> 00:16:50.408
As a final step, connect this with what we were doing earlier,

00:16:50.408 --> 00:16:53.464
where we constructed that strange Frank Gehry-looking surface,

00:16:53.464 --> 00:16:56.180
encoding all of the loop pair data that we cared about.

00:16:56.780 --> 00:17:00.870
Now, you also know that unordered pairs of points have this natural correspondence

00:17:00.870 --> 00:17:04.960
with a Möbius strip, in the sense that you have a two-way continuous association.

00:17:05.460 --> 00:17:09.156
What that means is there's necessarily a continuous function

00:17:09.156 --> 00:17:12.420
from the Möbius strip onto this surface in 3D space.

00:17:12.800 --> 00:17:16.164
To animate this, let me show you what it looks like if I move every

00:17:16.164 --> 00:17:19.929
point of that surface back to its corresponding point on the Möbius strip,

00:17:19.929 --> 00:17:22.640
the one associated with the same pair of loop points.

00:17:23.599 --> 00:17:26.093
And here it is going the other way, very explicitly

00:17:26.093 --> 00:17:28.880
showing the map from the Möbius strip onto this surface.

00:17:31.460 --> 00:17:33.260
But it's not just any map.

00:17:33.560 --> 00:17:36.040
Focus one last time on the gun hanging on the wall.

00:17:36.740 --> 00:17:39.530
The edge of this Möbius strip, which, remember,

00:17:39.530 --> 00:17:42.796
corresponds to all the pairs that look like x comma x,

00:17:42.796 --> 00:17:48.020
that has to land on the loop itself, meaning it has to end up confined to the xy-plane.

00:17:48.640 --> 00:17:53.263
And if you take a moment and play around with the idea in your head of Möbius strips

00:17:53.263 --> 00:17:57.885
and how they can fit into 3D space, you might find it entirely believable that it's

00:17:57.885 --> 00:18:02.618
impossible to embed a Möbius strip into 3D in such a way that its edge stays confined

00:18:02.618 --> 00:18:06.800
to a plane like this, at least not without somehow crossing through itself.

00:18:07.520 --> 00:18:10.260
If this claim is true, we have our desired result.

00:18:10.260 --> 00:18:13.673
Self-intersection in this context means two distinct points

00:18:13.673 --> 00:18:17.260
from the strip have to land on the same point of the surface.

00:18:17.880 --> 00:18:20.826
And in turn, that means there's two distinct pairs of points that have

00:18:20.826 --> 00:18:23.940
the same midpoint and the same distance, and hence they form a rectangle.

00:18:25.280 --> 00:18:28.010
While this claim is very believable, proving this kind

00:18:28.010 --> 00:18:30.740
of statement from scratch can be a little bit tricky.

00:18:31.520 --> 00:18:35.860
In fact, not only is it tricky, but stated so far the claim is not even true.

00:18:36.200 --> 00:18:40.787
After the first edition of this lesson, the mathematician Dan Asimov reached out to me,

00:18:40.787 --> 00:18:45.373
a construction that he made for embedding a Möbius strip into three dimensions in such

00:18:45.373 --> 00:18:49.960
a way that its boundary not only ends up on a plane, but it ends up equaling a circle.

00:18:50.760 --> 00:18:52.320
Here, that's a little bit of a mind warp.

00:18:52.500 --> 00:18:53.900
Let me play it for you one more time.

00:18:56.240 --> 00:18:58.974
For me at least, I find this very trippy to think about,

00:18:58.974 --> 00:19:03.320
and even after seeing the transition, I have trouble getting my brain to parse that what

00:19:03.320 --> 00:19:04.980
I'm looking at is a Möbius strip.

00:19:05.420 --> 00:19:06.700
But that's what it is.

00:19:06.700 --> 00:19:10.974
The more familiar strip-with-a-twist shape that we often see is just one out of

00:19:10.974 --> 00:19:15.680
infinitely many ways that you could represent what this mathematical object really is.

00:19:16.100 --> 00:19:19.480
This bizarre snail-shell looking thing is no less valid.

00:19:20.560 --> 00:19:23.824
The existence of an embedding like this would provide a fatal

00:19:23.824 --> 00:19:27.569
counterexample to our desired result, but notice how in this example,

00:19:27.569 --> 00:19:31.100
the interior of the surface goes both above and below the circle.

00:19:31.380 --> 00:19:34.049
But in the surface we constructed with the loop pairs,

00:19:34.049 --> 00:19:37.460
all of the points are necessarily above the xy plane, by definition.

00:19:37.980 --> 00:19:41.680
So what we really want to prove is that it's impossible to map a

00:19:41.680 --> 00:19:46.189
Möbius strip into 3D in such a way that its edge is confined to the xy plane,

00:19:46.189 --> 00:19:49.600
and the interior of the strip is strictly above the plane.

00:19:49.980 --> 00:19:53.700
If that's impossible, at least not without self-intersection, we have our result.

00:19:54.460 --> 00:19:56.971
Again, proving this rigorously from scratch can be tricky,

00:19:56.971 --> 00:20:00.261
but what I can do is offer a connection to something that many of you might

00:20:00.261 --> 00:20:01.040
have seen before.

00:20:01.040 --> 00:20:05.121
So far, what we know is that this strange surface is really a Möbius strip,

00:20:05.121 --> 00:20:09.420
and I want you to consider the reflection of the surface underneath the plane.

00:20:10.060 --> 00:20:15.200
Taken together, this surface and its reflection underneath form some new closed surface.

00:20:15.640 --> 00:20:18.014
Essentially, it's whatever you get if you glue the

00:20:18.014 --> 00:20:20.340
edge of one Möbius strip to the edge of another.

00:20:20.740 --> 00:20:22.858
So this raises the question, what do you get when

00:20:22.858 --> 00:20:25.020
you glue together the edges of two Möbius strips?

00:20:27.280 --> 00:20:30.750
Well, one really nice way to think about this is to look back at the diagram that

00:20:30.750 --> 00:20:34.220
we had earlier, the one that we eventually cut and glued to be the strip itself.

00:20:34.660 --> 00:20:38.553
If you take a reflection of that, giving us another thing that will become

00:20:38.553 --> 00:20:42.340
a Möbius strip, you can nicely glue these together along that red edge.

00:20:42.780 --> 00:20:44.778
To figure out what surface this represents, you

00:20:44.778 --> 00:20:46.860
basically do the same trick that we did earlier.

00:20:47.180 --> 00:20:50.698
You first cut along this other diagonal, recording the new cut with

00:20:50.698 --> 00:20:54.320
some new colored arrows, reminding us how we're going to glue later.

00:20:54.820 --> 00:20:57.776
This lets you flip one of the pieces so that we could glue,

00:20:57.776 --> 00:20:59.680
for example, the teal edges together.

00:21:00.220 --> 00:21:03.880
And what you're left with is something very similar to the torus diagram.

00:21:04.360 --> 00:21:07.263
You can start the same way, where you curl it around to glue,

00:21:07.263 --> 00:21:09.880
for example, those pink edges together, making a tube.

00:21:10.300 --> 00:21:13.153
But the difference between this and a torus is that those

00:21:13.153 --> 00:21:16.257
circular ends have opposite orientations, which makes it kind

00:21:16.257 --> 00:21:19.460
of awkward to glue them together, maintaining that orientation.

00:21:19.900 --> 00:21:24.270
One way that you could do this is to pass one side of that tube through the tube itself

00:21:24.270 --> 00:21:28.440
so that it can meet up with the other end kind of coming from the other direction.

00:21:29.020 --> 00:21:31.240
What you get as a result is known as a Klein bottle.

00:21:31.640 --> 00:21:34.790
This is kind of a celebrity shape in math, very famous because

00:21:34.790 --> 00:21:37.940
of the bizarre way that it has no clear interior or exterior.

00:21:38.360 --> 00:21:40.516
Any point that seems to be on the inside could be

00:21:40.516 --> 00:21:42.760
moved around in some way to end up on the outside.

00:21:43.220 --> 00:21:46.162
If you know about Klein bottles, one of the main things you might

00:21:46.162 --> 00:21:49.104
know is that it's impossible to properly represent them in three

00:21:49.104 --> 00:21:52.000
dimensions without somehow having the surface intersect itself.

00:21:52.420 --> 00:21:54.660
In higher dimensions, it can live much more comfortably,

00:21:54.660 --> 00:21:56.700
but down here there's just no way to make it work.

00:21:57.180 --> 00:22:00.740
This is a more general fact about any closed, non-orientable surface.

00:22:01.300 --> 00:22:04.438
And what's very satisfying in my opinion is how, for us,

00:22:04.438 --> 00:22:07.520
right here, this fact is relevant for an actual proof.

00:22:07.920 --> 00:22:12.670
If you believe that Klein bottles can't be represented in 3D without self-intersection,

00:22:12.670 --> 00:22:15.727
it means that the surface we constructed over our loop,

00:22:15.727 --> 00:22:20.040
viewed together with its reflection, must have some kind of self-intersection.

00:22:20.480 --> 00:22:23.859
And based on the construction of the surface, that means we have two distinct pairs

00:22:23.859 --> 00:22:27.320
of points with the same midpoint and the same distance apart, and hence a rectangle.

00:22:28.880 --> 00:22:33.568
For the extra curious among you, I'll leave up on screen an argument for why exactly

00:22:33.568 --> 00:22:38.200
any closed, non-orientable surface cannot exist in 3D without intersecting itself.

00:22:39.140 --> 00:22:43.339
Stepping back though, I want to talk a little bit more about the unsolved problem,

00:22:43.339 --> 00:22:46.974
proving that any loop necessarily has not only an inscribed rectangle,

00:22:46.974 --> 00:22:48.920
but specifically an inscribed square.

00:22:49.560 --> 00:22:52.041
Given the entire proof that we just went through,

00:22:52.041 --> 00:22:55.838
a natural thought that you might have is to focus not only on the midpoint

00:22:55.838 --> 00:22:58.318
and the distance between a given pair of points,

00:22:58.318 --> 00:23:00.900
but to also record the angle of that line segment.

00:23:01.260 --> 00:23:05.651
Because if you had two such line segments that not only share a midpoint and a length,

00:23:05.651 --> 00:23:09.940
but also differ by 90 degrees, that would necessarily mean that they form a square.

00:23:10.440 --> 00:23:14.196
If sitting there watching this right now, that gives you an instinct to somehow

00:23:14.196 --> 00:23:16.953
think about embedding Möbius strips into four dimensions,

00:23:16.953 --> 00:23:19.520
since this now has four numbers worth of information,

00:23:19.520 --> 00:23:22.040
well then you would have very good instincts indeed.

00:23:22.680 --> 00:23:26.250
In 2020, the mathematicians Joshua Green and Andrew Lobb proved

00:23:26.250 --> 00:23:30.670
a really nice extension of this result in the specific case of smooth curves,

00:23:30.670 --> 00:23:33.560
which is contrasted with rough ones like fractals.

00:23:34.100 --> 00:23:37.016
I'll talk a little bit more about smoothness in a moment,

00:23:37.016 --> 00:23:40.137
but what Green and Lobb showed was how in this special case,

00:23:40.137 --> 00:23:44.230
not only can you always find a square that had actually been known for a while,

00:23:44.230 --> 00:23:47.300
but you can find rectangles of every possible aspect ratio.

00:23:47.840 --> 00:23:51.412
And if you look into their paper, which I'll link to in the description,

00:23:51.412 --> 00:23:55.529
it involves discussion of embedding Möbius strips and Klein bottles into a certain

00:23:55.529 --> 00:23:56.720
four-dimensional space.

00:23:57.280 --> 00:24:01.011
And while that would sound just weird and kind of wild without any background,

00:24:01.011 --> 00:24:05.172
hopefully now you have an instinct for why that kind of approach is not only sensible,

00:24:05.172 --> 00:24:06.320
but utterly reasonable.

00:24:07.100 --> 00:24:10.953
If you understand the rectangle proof, the one that we walked through in this video,

00:24:10.953 --> 00:24:14.760
you can also have some instinct for why smoothness would make the question easier.

00:24:15.340 --> 00:24:19.367
What smoothness means in a technical sense is that you can take as many derivatives

00:24:19.367 --> 00:24:23.104
of your curve as you want, but geometrically one of the bits of significance

00:24:23.104 --> 00:24:26.840
here is that for smooth curves, every point has a well-defined tangent line.

00:24:27.400 --> 00:24:30.703
Now in our proof, think about how much it mattered that we had clean

00:24:30.703 --> 00:24:34.200
limiting behavior as the pair of points got closer and closer together.

00:24:34.500 --> 00:24:36.140
This was the gun hanging on the wall.

00:24:36.360 --> 00:24:39.690
It was the constraint for where the edge of the Möbius strip had to land,

00:24:39.690 --> 00:24:41.560
which was critical for the final result.

00:24:42.020 --> 00:24:44.810
Now if in addition to tracking the midpoint and distance,

00:24:44.810 --> 00:24:47.992
you're also tracking the angle of that line connecting the pair,

00:24:47.992 --> 00:24:51.614
then for smooth curves the angle also has some clean limiting behavior as

00:24:51.614 --> 00:24:55.383
the pair gets close together, namely it approaches whatever the angle of the

00:24:55.383 --> 00:24:56.900
tangent line at that point is.

00:24:57.300 --> 00:24:59.714
But for a rough curve, something like a fractal,

00:24:59.714 --> 00:25:02.380
there might not be limiting behavior for that angle.

00:25:02.800 --> 00:25:05.338
What really makes the inscribed square problem hard,

00:25:05.338 --> 00:25:09.340
the reason it remains unsolved, is the case of all of the rough curves like this.

00:25:10.160 --> 00:25:13.892
The main thing I want you to take away from this lesson is a sense that when

00:25:13.892 --> 00:25:18.115
mathematicians talk about shapes like Möbius strips or Klein bottles or even stranger

00:25:18.115 --> 00:25:22.240
higher dimensional objects, they aren't just studying bizarreness for its own sake.

00:25:22.680 --> 00:25:26.331
Sometimes when you're solving a problem and you're coming up with structures

00:25:26.331 --> 00:25:30.511
to help you in that process, some of those structures are very mind-bending to ponder,

00:25:30.511 --> 00:25:33.921
and sometimes they're so fun to think about that those objects trickle

00:25:33.921 --> 00:25:36.900
their way out of research and into recreational math outlets.

00:25:37.260 --> 00:25:39.941
And sometimes in the process the original problem-solving

00:25:39.941 --> 00:25:41.540
relevance gets a little bit lost.

00:25:42.280 --> 00:25:44.866
Another takeaway I hope you have is that a Möbius

00:25:44.866 --> 00:25:47.400
strip is not really any one particular surface.

00:25:47.920 --> 00:25:51.131
This, this, and this are also Möbius strips, as is the

00:25:51.131 --> 00:25:54.640
more abstract idea of unordered pairs of points on a loop.

00:25:55.260 --> 00:25:57.388
Another case where that one might come up is in

00:25:57.388 --> 00:25:59.380
describing every possible musical interval.

00:26:00.020 --> 00:26:03.074
This is getting at the idea of a topological space,

00:26:03.074 --> 00:26:07.985
which is not so much a particular shape as it is an infinite family of shapes all

00:26:07.985 --> 00:26:10.740
connected by a certain notion of equivalence.

00:26:11.540 --> 00:26:15.255
Defining what exactly a topological space is, and what topology is for that matter,

00:26:15.255 --> 00:26:18.926
is a little bit subtle, and it's one of those things where the formal definitions

00:26:18.926 --> 00:26:21.880
don't really shed a lot of light on it, at least not immediately.

00:26:22.440 --> 00:26:26.196
But one way to describe what topology is about is that it's a game

00:26:26.196 --> 00:26:29.440
of understanding continuous associations between things,

00:26:29.440 --> 00:26:33.480
and understanding what is or is not possible under those associations.

00:26:33.980 --> 00:26:37.099
All of the famous shapes from this field of study are better

00:26:37.099 --> 00:26:40.321
thought of as representatives of huge families of shapes that

00:26:40.321 --> 00:26:43.960
all have essentially the same kind of behavior under continuous maps.

00:26:44.520 --> 00:26:47.319
And the reason that mathematicians get really excited about

00:26:47.319 --> 00:26:50.260
bizarre properties and impossibilities is not just aesthetic.

00:26:50.640 --> 00:26:53.500
It's because when you're looking for logical proofs,

00:26:53.500 --> 00:26:56.800
constraints and impossibilities are your fuel for progress.

00:26:58.760 --> 00:27:02.295
If you enjoy this kind of video, there's a playlist I put together on this channel

00:27:02.295 --> 00:27:05.960
full of a lot of other neat proofs and puzzle solutions that come out of left field.

